{
  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "t01-q01",
  "speechText": "Basically, Node.js runs JavaScript on the main event loop. With I O, like database or network requests, Node.js can wait without blocking the main thread. When I use async and await, only the current function pauses, while the event loop can continue handling other work. Some file and DNS operations can also use the libuv worker pool. CPU-heavy work is different. If I run a heavy calculation on the main thread, it can block the event loop, so other requests have to wait. For CPU-heavy work, I can use worker threads, a background queue, or a separate service, depending on the use case. So overall, Node.js works very well for I O-heavy systems, but I avoid running heavy CPU work directly on the main thread.",
